The '''History of Guajicutea (1392–1520)''' covers the period from the Treaty of Kariwári (1392), which ended the [[Kari Wirá]] (Sea Hurricane War), through the arrival of [[Empire of Burawa|Burawi]] explorer [[Guled Badweyn]] on 5 December 1520. The post-war reconstruction produced a trade boom, the development of the first large ocean-going vessels (the [[Kanu Maku]] class), the independent discovery of bronze metallurgy, and the only direct voyage to the [[Aruanãyára Empire]].

== Post-war reconstruction (1392–1405) ==

=== The Treaty of Kariwári ===

The [[Kari Wirá]] ended in Y876, Month of Long Nights (1392 CE) with the Treaty of Kariwári ("Sea Peace"). The Tapuyic Empire withdrew from the central Capuyaquiran; the western trade route was reopened to both parties; the Boraku islands remained Guaji tributaries; no tribute or prisoners were exchanged.

The war had lasted nearly fifty years (Y828–Y876, 1344–1392 CE). The port of Yukubá had been raided twice. Hundreds of warriors were dead. The western trade route to the Tapuyan mainland had been severed for decades. The population had dropped from approximately 35,000 to 30,000.

=== Bika Kuru's reconstruction ===

Bika Kuru (ruler 35, reigned 1378–1405), the king who had commanded the counter-offensive, devoted the remainder of his reign to reconstruction. His regnal name meant "Path Rest."

The sewn-plank war canoe fleet built during the conflict (Y864–Y870) was kept in service. The shipbuilding yards at Waribá and Barikú, established during the war, became permanent institutions — producing both war and trade vessels throughout the post-war period. The western trade route from Yukubá through the Lesser Capuyaquiran to the Tapuyan mainland was reopened. Yukubá, raided twice during the war, received stone-and-limestone fortifications replacing the wooden defenses that had failed. Watchtowers were built along the southwest coast.

Bika Kuru also commissioned prospecting for copper in the volcanic highlands. The Tapuyic forces had carried copper and bronze weapons during the war, while the Guaji fought with stone, bone, and hardwood. Copper deposits were found in {{w|porphyry copper}} and {{w|volcanogenic massive sulfide|volcanogenic sulfide}} settings in the northern mountains. Mining, smelting, and hot-working were new skills; the first locally-mined copper appeared during the late 1390s, but output was small for decades.

By the end of Bika Kuru's reign in 1405, the population had rebounded to approximately 34,000.

== The golden age (1405–1458) ==

=== Guani Buka — "Open Gold" (1405–1432) ===

Bika Kuru was succeeded by Guani Buka (ruler 36, reigned 1405–1432). His regnal name, "Open Gold," described the trade expansion of the period.

==== Trade boom ====

With the western route restored and the tributary system solidified, Guaji trade expanded. The larger sewn-plank trade canoes carried bulk cargo on regular routes. Exports included gold ornaments, volcanic stone tools ({{w|obsidian}}, {{w|andesite}}), pottery, dried fish, {{w|cassava}} flour, {{w|tobacco}}, and {{w|sulfur}}. Imports included {{w|cotton}} and textiles, {{w|wool}} and camelid fiber from Tapuya, shells and pearls from atoll islands, specialty woods, and marriage partners.

Gold from [[Mina Yaruma]] was the highest-value export. The trade balance favored Guajicutea: high-value, low-weight gold ornaments exchanged for bulk materials the island lacked.

==== Introduction of maize ====

Around Y890–Y900 (1405–1420 CE), {{w|maize}} was introduced to Guajicutea through Tapuyan trade. Maize was a mainland cereal grain that arrived via the western trade route. It grew well on the drier western leeward side of the island, supplementing but not replacing the root-crop complex of cassava, yams, and [[makú]]. By the time of the Boraku Revolt (Y905), maize was established in western Guajicutea.

==== The Kanu Maku ====

Guaji shipbuilding had evolved through several stages: single-log dugout canoes in the pre-unification period, sewn-plank trade canoes from the 500s onward, and sewn-plank war canoes developed during the Kari Wirá. The war canoes were long and narrow, designed for speed and boarding. The Kanu Maku class, developed in the 1400s, was a different category — broader, deeper, sailing vessels rather than paddled ones.

{{main|Pre-colonial shipbuilding in Guajicutea}}

The key innovation was a wood joinery technique called [[Yabikú]] — interlocking carved joints (dovetails, stepped laps, tapered mortise-tenon) that tightened under load. No iron fasteners were used; wooden pegs and dowels reinforced the joints. The technique was developed specifically for the Kanu Maku project, where fiber stitching alone was insufficient for a hull of this size. The availability of copper axes from the 1390s onward also made the project feasible — copper tools allowed more precise shaping of hardwood planks than stone tools alone.

Kanu Maku specifications: 15–20 meters length, 3–4 meters beam, outrigger stabilization, cotton sail, steering oar, 3–5 tons cargo capacity, 6–10 crew, 3–5 years construction time per vessel. Master craftsmen (yabiku-kú) required years of apprenticeship.

Three Kanu Maku were built: a cargo vessel based at Yukubá, a cargo vessel based at Waribá, and a warship, the Kanu Wirá, later retrofitted from a cargo hull. The Kanu Wirá was used to suppress the Boraku Revolt. The Waribá vessel was lost in 1502 when Captain [[Kari Buka]] sailed it north to the [[Aruanãyára Empire]] and it crashed on the coast of [[Lacashe]].

The Kanu Maku was developed partly to enable a direct northern voyage to the [[Aruanãyára Empire]], bypassing the Tapuyic middlemen who controlled the tin trade. The open-sea route required a vessel large enough to carry provisions for weeks and sturdy enough for open-ocean conditions.

==== The Boraku Revolt (1421–1424) ====

The [[Boraku islands]], which had been the front line of the Kari Wirá, were still recovering from the war. Their canoes had been destroyed, their populations depleted, their food supply disrupted. The tribute demands continued as if the war had never happened.

In Y905, Month of First Rains (1421 CE), the Boraku chiefs refused the annual tribute. A Guaji tribute collector (a nitaino) was killed on a Boraku island. The [[Murani islands]] joined the revolt.

Guani Buka ordered the punitive fleet. At the Battle of Karibári (Y906, Month of Still Sea, 1422 CE), the sewn-plank war fleet engaged the combined Boraku-Murani canoe force near the Boraku islands. The sewn-plank war canoes outmaneuvered and overwhelmed the tributary dugouts. The revolt fleet was crushed.

The revolt was fully suppressed by Y908 (1424 CE). New terms were imposed: increased tribute quotas, stationed nitaino garrisons on the largest Boraku and Murani islands, children of chiefs sent to Guajicutea as political hostages, and annual tribute collection reinforced with armed escort.

=== Warí Kuru — Cultural flowering (1432–1458) ===

Guani Buka was succeeded by Warí Kuru (ruler 37, reigned 1432–1458). The Warí (high priestess) during this period held particular authority; the [[Catacumbas del Abismo|calendar corridor]] was expanded during these decades. The population grew toward approximately 37,000. Stone carving, pottery, featherwork, and gold ornamentation were widely practiced.

==== Bronze metallurgy ====

Bronze — tin-copper alloying — was discovered in the late 1450s (approximately Y939–Y942). This was independently developed in Adonia. Guaji smiths found that certain ores, when heated with copper, produced a harder metal.

Tin was the limiting factor. Copper was available in the volcanic highlands, but tin was not found locally. Tin was imported through Tapuyic trade — the Tapuyic controlled the mainland routes to the Aruanãyára Empire, where tin sources existed. The scarcity of tin limited bronze production.

By the end of Warí Kuru's reign, bronze was used for armor (breastplates, arm guards, helmets for high-ranking nitainos), ornamentation (armbands, chest discs for caciques), and some bronze-edged weapons for the highest-ranking warriors. Most fighters still used stone and hardwood.

== Decline and peak (1458–1500) ==

=== Bari Kuru — Increasing storms (1458–1480) ===

Bari Kuru (ruler 38, reigned 1458–1480) inherited a wealthy but increasingly strained kingdom. The storm ledger in the catacombs shows an increasing frequency of severe weather during this period. The tributary system weakened. The garrisons stationed on the Boraku and Murani islands after the revolt became harder to maintain. Some tributaries drifted away through neglect as Guajicutea could no longer project power to the outer islands consistently.

In 1478, the [[Tapuyic Empire]] dissolved into the [[Tapuya Confederacy]], a union of states. The main confederacy member the Guaji dealt with was the [[Tapuri Kingdom]], which controlled the islands and coasts of the western Capuyaquiran. The dissolution ended the centralized empire that had fought the [[Kari Wirá]] and replaced it with a looser political structure.

The population continued to grow, reaching approximately 38,000 by 1450. The bronze industry produced the visual hierarchy of bronze-armored officers leading stone-armed warriors that characterized the Guaji military by 1500.

=== Yabí Tabá (1480–1538) ===

Yabí Tabá (ruler 39, reigned 1480–1538) was the last ruler of the pre-colonial era. Under Yabí Tabá, the population reached its pre-contact peak of approximately 40,000 — the highest in Guaji history. Yabí Tabá died during the [[Great Pestilence]] of the 1530s, and his death contributed to the political upheaval that followed.

The settlement system was fully developed. Three main ports — Yukubá (2,500–3,500), Waribá (2,000–3,000), and Barikú (1,500–2,500) — anchored a network of secondary ports and rural settlements. Approximately 70–78% of the population lived in dispersed rural or jungle settlements, 17–24% in coastal centers, and 5–8% in religious or mountain communities.

The military had a visual hierarchy: bronze-armored nitainos and war chiefs leading warriors with traditional stone and wood weapons. The fleet included sewn-plank war canoes and the three Kanu Maku vessels.

== Domestic works and cultural production (1500–1520) ==

The period from 1500 to 1520 was one of prolonged peace. No major conflict had occurred since the [[Boraku Revolt]] ended in 1424 — nearly a century of internal stability. The population was at its peak, the catacombs were active, and the calendar corridor was still being carved.

As the population continued to grow, agriculture intensified. Larger plantation farms developed in the fertile central river valleys and the western leeward lowlands, replacing the smaller slash-and-burn plots of earlier periods. The increasing availability of copper tools — axes, adzes, and clearing implements — made land clearing and soil preparation more efficient than with stone tools alone. Copper had been mined since the 1390s, and by the early 1500s copper tools were widespread enough to shift agricultural practice.

This long peace produced a body of domestic works — the term "cultural revolution" has been applied to the output of this period in later scholarship. The specific works and developments of this period are still being studied.

=== The Kari Bika ===

By the 1300s, generations of Guaji explorers and traders had compiled the [[Kari Bika]] ("sea marks") — bark cloth charts and carved wooden tablets showing the known islands, trade routes, and coastlines of the Capuyaquiran Sea and the Tapuyan mainland. The Kari Bika included an approximation of land to the north of the Warabi islands, deduced from bird migrations, cloud formations, driftwood, and current patterns. The Kari Bika gave the Guaji the geographic basis to plan the northern voyage.

=== Kari Buka's return and its impact ===

In 1502, Captain [[Kari Buka]] sailed the Waribá-based Kanu Maku north in the first and only direct voyage to the [[Aruanãyára Empire]], seeking to bypass the Tapuyic middlemen who controlled the tin trade. The ship crashed on the coast of [[Lacashe]] after a 280 km open-sea crossing. Kari Buka returned to Guajicutea in 1514 — twelve years later — by traveling south through Tapuyic territory. The chronicle of the voyage, the [[Kari Yabí]] ("The Hidden Sea"), was transcribed to paper in the 1530s while Kari Buka was still alive, making it one of the best-preserved Guaji records. See [[Kari Yabí]] for the full account.

Kari Buka was still alive in 1516 when the first Burawi vessels were sighted, and in 1520 when Guled Badweyn made first contact. As the only Guaji who had seen the northern mainland firsthand, he was likely consulted by the political elite.

== Arrival of the Burawi (1516–1520) ==

In early 1516, the [[Empire of Burawa]] established the [[Lacashe]] colony on the northern coast of [[Abyala]], on former [[Aruanãyára Empire|Aruanãyára]] territory — the same coast where Kari Buka's Kanu Maku had crashed fourteen years earlier. By April 1516, word reached Guajicutea of large vessels sighted in the northern waters. Coastal watchtowers recorded ships larger than anything in the Capuyaquiran Sea. The political elite — the Cacique Má Yabí Tabá, the Warí, and the council of caciques — understood these to be people of a far-off tribe seeking trade. Reports described an empire from the sea that had taken land from the Aruanãyára.

On 5 December 1520, a Burawi expedition led by [[Guled Badweyn]] arrived at Guajicutea. Guled had been conducting a trade and exploration mission with the islands off the coast of Lacashe, making his way west through the Capuyaquiran archipelago. His directive from the Empire of Burawa was to assess lands and armies for possibilities of trade or expansion. Communication was attempted using a translator kidnapped from the Lacashe colony, who likely spoke an Aruanãyára or Lacashe dialect rather than Guaji; communication was difficult.

Guled's troops left gold coins and maize as gifts. The gold coins were minted currency, a concept the Guaji did not have. The maize was familiar — the Guaji had been growing it for nearly a century through Tapuyan trade. In his notes, Guled recorded that the Guaji coastal cities were the largest he had seen so far — he had not yet reached Tapuya — and described them as "cosmopolitan," resembling the smaller port cities of Burawa but with much smaller vessels. He departed the following day.

